etion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":5,\"status\":\"completed\"}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"name\":\"Combined search\",\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":7,\"name\":\"Embeddings search\",\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":8,\"name\":\"Keywords search\",\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":9,\"name\":\"Page image search\",\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":8,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":0,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.73] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"The OWL ontology browser and editor called Swoop [30] was cre- ated at the American University of Maryland. The W3C website, de- scribed it as a compact and straightforward ontology editor, fully im- plemented and accessible for OWL. It has a web-oriented approach and includes many common browser features: address bar, history buttons, bookmarks and hypertext navigation. The primary panel of the appli- cation, which is divided into two tabs, is where most of the work is done. The Ontology Info panel provides general details about it, such as name, annotations, entity counts, and statistics on entity traits. The Species Validation panel depicts each entity and describes its traits or the nature it assumed in the ontology (class, property or individual). Three di\\ufb00erent display options are available for the ontology entities. One can create a comprehensive list of all existing entities, arranged alphabeti- cally, or can examine class or attribute hierarchies, proposed with a tree\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=5\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":8,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":1,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.78] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Swoop has Blackbox, a method to diagnose entities. Reasoners are tasked to develop a set of domains, in this method; the ontology struc- ture is used to pinpoint a problem\\u2019s root cause. In case of con\\ufb02ict, the program displays the event reason in the o\\ufb00ending entity description \\ufb01eld with the use of a semi-formal language and the insertion of some accountable axioms. Here, we can use the Run Debug Tests, Debugging / Explanation, and Repair Ontology commands in the Advanced tab. We can use Version Control to maintain our ontologies appropriately and to verify that their entities are used correctly. This tool o\\ufb00ers two sorts of operations, changelogs and checkpoints; it allows users to keep vari- ous iterations of the same ontology. Changelogs describe the ontology\\u2019s evolutionary route and list changes and adjustments in chronological order. Checkpoints provide quick switching between versions, showcas- ing the range of current models. Swoop supports all RDFS/OWL rela- tional constructs\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=6\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":8,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":2,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.230] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"3.8.5. Documentation\\n\\nThe Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 user manual is relatively straightforward; it contains an introduction that explains all theoretical and conceptual aspects of ontologies and languages for the semantic web. This site has produced good, comprehensive updated work, becoming thus a great resource for all Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 users. Swoop, OntoStudio and TopBraid Composer (FE) ed- itors provide documentation ontology and ontology libraries. A user\\u2019s guide to the program is provided by Fluent Editor, VonBench and OBO- Edit. The huge and well-organized materials are made possible by frame- works with hypertext links.\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=8\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":8,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":3,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.79] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"in order to transmit entities to various ontologies. To reuse entities already present in other works, users are thus required to employ links rather than pure importations. Swoop does not currently support trading with shares, but a \\u201dsimple copy-paste \\u201d could provide a workaround.\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=6\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":8,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":4,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.92] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"3.8. Software comparison\\n\\nWe now provide a general ODEs comparison. Table 3 collects gen- eral discussed tools features. Table 4 collects tools aspects of Usability, Scalability, Stability, Integration, Documentation and Originality. Each characteristic is evaluated with a score ranging from a minimum of 1 to a maximum of 3 yellow stars.\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=7\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":8,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":5,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.99] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"matter are available on its Standard (SE) and Maestro Editions (ME). Fluent editor provides two plug-ins viz., a Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 interoperability plug- e in and an Rplug-in using the ROntorionpakages to organize the ontology l b a content. VocBench3 support plugins which provide additional capabili- T\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=7\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":8,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":6,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.89] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"of fatal \\ufb02aws that could jeopardize accuracy. Checks are performed automatically during very speci\\ufb01c stages of the ontology\\u2019s life, such as when it is loaded, when text \\ufb01elds are changed and while using the Text Editor, that also keeps track of feedback on the actions taken. There are many di\\ufb00erent control studies, that range from the examination of the name of terms and associated IDs, to glancing for synonyms and dis- junction features. If a user needs to manually engage with the program control system he must utilize the Veri\\ufb01cation Manager. The window contains a list of all kinds of inspection allowed. Finally, we mention the idea of a cross product, also present in Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 and Swoop, but with a somewhat di\\ufb00erent meaning in this situation. In OBO-Edit Cross Prod- uct represents the intersection or relations among the classes that ensure the identi\\ufb01cation of all the hierarchical correspondences within the on- tology. In Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 and Swoop,\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=6\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":8,\"name\":\" [0.01s]\",\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":8,\"status\":\"completed\"}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":7,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":0,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.63] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"OntoStudio 3.2.0 [27] has been the widely used and accepted tool for building and maintaining ontologies a few years ago [106] . Its prede- cessor version was called OntoEdit [131] . It is a multilingual editor and ontology modeler with a lot of customization options. French, English and German are the languages that can de\\ufb01ne synonyms. Classes, prop- erties, rules, queries and mapping are all part of it. This editor supports the OWL, RDF and ObjectLogic formats [27] . When starting a project, users have the option to choose their preferred storage type, includ- ing internal repositories or a collaborative server. Di\\ufb00erent \\ufb01le formats, such as xml, html, doc, ppt, pdf and postscript, can store data. With the use of a query builder, it provides the option to get data from databases, such as a SQL database. The availability of Query Builder SQL makes it simple to design queries. An external OntoStudio support tool called On- toBroker uses ObjectLogic and SPARQL to query the graph. SPARQL\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=5\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":7,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":1,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.230] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"3.8.5. Documentation\\n\\nThe Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 user manual is relatively straightforward; it contains an introduction that explains all theoretical and conceptual aspects of ontologies and languages for the semantic web. This site has produced good, comprehensive updated work, becoming thus a great resource for all Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 users. Swoop, OntoStudio and TopBraid Composer (FE) ed- itors provide documentation ontology and ontology libraries. A user\\u2019s guide to the program is provided by Fluent Editor, VonBench and OBO- Edit. The huge and well-organized materials are made possible by frame- works with hypertext links.\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=8\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":7,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":2,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.52] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"3.1. Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9\\n\\nThe Stanford Center for Biomedical Informatics Research developed Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 [128] , an open-source ontology editor and knowledge store. It is a suitable instrument for editing ontologies since it gives users a thorough understanding of the OWL ontology structure. In the current landscape of ontology editors, it is the most complete and popular op- tion. The National Institute of General Medical Sciences supports it as a national resource for biomedical ontologies and knowledge bases. We list some activity modules below.\\n\\n1 Active ontology: enables the development of a brand-new ontology or the opening of an existing one by specifying the directory that it addresses (ontology IRI) or the URI (Ontology Version IRI). The associated source codes in the RDF/XML and OWL/XML languages can be examined and we can reorganize this data.\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=4\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":7,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":3,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.10] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"responding to the above proposition. Such ontologies are created and maintained by scientists to support the retrieval, integration and anal- ysis of their data. The issues pertaining to ontology development, map- ping and maintenance are critical key areas that must be comprehended and solved [24] . The thesis to be defended is that life science ontolo- gies, here extensively explored, provide unequaled support for scienti\\ufb01c study, and it is simple and intuitive for scientists to create or integrate ontologies. In this paper, we have been investigating and sharing e\\ufb03- cient tools for achieving and building ontologies. We discuss the use of ontology developments (ODEs) from textual scienti\\ufb01c resources, such as Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 [25] , Topbraid Composer [26] , Ontostudio [27] , Fluent Edi- tor [28] , VocBench [29] , Swoop [30] , and Obo-edit [31] . We begin by listing some available open source biomedical ontologies ( Table 1 ) and bioinformatic databases ( Table 2 ).\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=1\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":7,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":4,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.245] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Fig. 7. OntoGraph relationships.\\n\\ngeneral characteristics and common knowledge regarding experimental studies, as well as to allow the communication among users, opening the opportunity to explore e-science concepts into the LS experimenta- tion domain. Potential future research directions would be to automate the process of creating the ontology. Beyond the creation of new on- tologies, another challenging area of work in the subject of ontologies is the evolution and mapping of current ontologies. Due to the cost of cre- ation, abstraction, and reusability, this is signi\\ufb01cant. Di\\ufb00erent types of ontologies can be derived from a core ontology to suit particular appli-\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=10\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":7,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":5,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.5] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Ontologies are used to support access to a multitude of databases that cover domains relevant information. Het- erogeneity and di\\ufb00erent semantics can be accessed by using structured texts and descriptions in a hierarchical concept de\\ufb01nition. We are interested in Life Sciences (LS) related ontologies including components taken from molecular biology, bioinformatics, physics, chemistry, medicine and other related areas. An Ontology comprises: (i) term connections, (ii) the identi\\ufb01cation of core concepts, (iii) data management, (iv) knowledge classi\\ufb01ca- tion and integration to collect key information. An ontology may be very useful in navigating through LS terms. This paper explores some available biomedical ontologies and frameworks. It describes the most common on- tology development environments (ODE): Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9, Topbraid Composer, Ontostudio, Fluent Editor, VocBench, Swoop and Obo-edit, to create ontologies from textual scienti\\ufb01c resources for LS plans. It also compares ontology\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=1\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":7,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":6,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.8] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Ontologies are increasingly being employed to enhance understand- ing and transferability of knowledge in Life Science [1\\u20137] . Research groups are interested in developing ontologies [8] to arrange and con- nect data into searchable libraries [ 9 , 10 ], to establish frameworks of essential concepts (e.g., OWS framework [11] ), to share information, to use knowledge to minimize confusion [12] and to allocate the grow- ing volume and data multiplicity in biomedical research, chemistry, medicine, genomics, biology and pharmacology [13] . Further reasons for appreciating its utility are a wider understanding of the value of standardized vocabularies and codi\\ufb01ed semantics in various domains [8] and the development or extension of construction tools. To enable widespread access [14] and give users the ability to publish, explore, create and update, a few works have been published regarding pro- cedures [15\\u201318] plans, criteria, activities and methodologies adapted to build them. The state of\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=1\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":7,\"name\":\" [0.03s]\",\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":7,\"status\":\"completed\"}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":9,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":0,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.232] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 9]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=9\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":9,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":1,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.233] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 9]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=9\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":9,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":2,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.234] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 9]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=9\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":9,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":3,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.235] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 9]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=9\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":9,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":4,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.236] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 9]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=9\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":9,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":5,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.237] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 9]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=9\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":9,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":6,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.238] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 9]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=9\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":9,\"name\":\" [0.04s]\",\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":9,\"status\":\"completed\"}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":0,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.230] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"3.8.5. Documentation\\n\\nThe Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 user manual is relatively straightforward; it contains an introduction that explains all theoretical and conceptual aspects of ontologies and languages for the semantic web. This site has produced good, comprehensive updated work, becoming thus a great resource for all Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 users. Swoop, OntoStudio and TopBraid Composer (FE) ed- itors provide documentation ontology and ontology libraries. A user\\u2019s guide to the program is provided by Fluent Editor, VonBench and OBO- Edit. The huge and well-organized materials are made possible by frame- works with hypertext links.\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=8\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":1,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.63] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"OntoStudio 3.2.0 [27] has been the widely used and accepted tool for building and maintaining ontologies a few years ago [106] . Its prede- cessor version was called OntoEdit [131] . It is a multilingual editor and ontology modeler with a lot of customization options. French, English and German are the languages that can de\\ufb01ne synonyms. Classes, prop- erties, rules, queries and mapping are all part of it. This editor supports the OWL, RDF and ObjectLogic formats [27] . When starting a project, users have the option to choose their preferred storage type, includ- ing internal repositories or a collaborative server. Di\\ufb00erent \\ufb01le formats, such as xml, html, doc, ppt, pdf and postscript, can store data. With the use of a query builder, it provides the option to get data from databases, such as a SQL database. The availability of Query Builder SQL makes it simple to design queries. An external OntoStudio support tool called On- toBroker uses ObjectLogic and SPARQL to query the graph. SPARQL\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=5\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":2,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.73] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"The OWL ontology browser and editor called Swoop [30] was cre- ated at the American University of Maryland. The W3C website, de- scribed it as a compact and straightforward ontology editor, fully im- plemented and accessible for OWL. It has a web-oriented approach and includes many common browser features: address bar, history buttons, bookmarks and hypertext navigation. The primary panel of the appli- cation, which is divided into two tabs, is where most of the work is done. The Ontology Info panel provides general details about it, such as name, annotations, entity counts, and statistics on entity traits. The Species Validation panel depicts each entity and describes its traits or the nature it assumed in the ontology (class, property or individual). Three di\\ufb00erent display options are available for the ontology entities. One can create a comprehensive list of all existing entities, arranged alphabeti- cally, or can examine class or attribute hierarchies, proposed with a tree\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=5\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":3,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.232] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 9]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=9\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":4,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.78] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Swoop has Blackbox, a method to diagnose entities. Reasoners are tasked to develop a set of domains, in this method; the ontology struc- ture is used to pinpoint a problem\\u2019s root cause. In case of con\\ufb02ict, the program displays the event reason in the o\\ufb00ending entity description \\ufb01eld with the use of a semi-formal language and the insertion of some accountable axioms. Here, we can use the Run Debug Tests, Debugging / Explanation, and Repair Ontology commands in the Advanced tab. We can use Version Control to maintain our ontologies appropriately and to verify that their entities are used correctly. This tool o\\ufb00ers two sorts of operations, changelogs and checkpoints; it allows users to keep vari- ous iterations of the same ontology. Changelogs describe the ontology\\u2019s evolutionary route and list changes and adjustments in chronological order. Checkpoints provide quick switching between versions, showcas- ing the range of current models. Swoop supports all RDFS/OWL rela- tional constructs\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=6\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":5,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.233] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 9]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=9\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":6,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.52] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"3.1. Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9\\n\\nThe Stanford Center for Biomedical Informatics Research developed Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 [128] , an open-source ontology editor and knowledge store. It is a suitable instrument for editing ontologies since it gives users a thorough understanding of the OWL ontology structure. In the current landscape of ontology editors, it is the most complete and popular op- tion. The National Institute of General Medical Sciences supports it as a national resource for biomedical ontologies and knowledge bases. We list some activity modules below.\\n\\n1 Active ontology: enables the development of a brand-new ontology or the opening of an existing one by specifying the directory that it addresses (ontology IRI) or the URI (Ontology Version IRI). The associated source codes in the RDF/XML and OWL/XML languages can be examined and we can reorganize this data.\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=4\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":7,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.234] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 9]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=9\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":8,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.10] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"responding to the above proposition. Such ontologies are created and maintained by scientists to support the retrieval, integration and anal- ysis of their data. The issues pertaining to ontology development, map- ping and maintenance are critical key areas that must be comprehended and solved [24] . The thesis to be defended is that life science ontolo- gies, here extensively explored, provide unequaled support for scienti\\ufb01c study, and it is simple and intuitive for scientists to create or integrate ontologies. In this paper, we have been investigating and sharing e\\ufb03- cient tools for achieving and building ontologies. We discuss the use of ontology developments (ODEs) from textual scienti\\ufb01c resources, such as Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 [25] , Topbraid Composer [26] , Ontostudio [27] , Fluent Edi- tor [28] , VocBench [29] , Swoop [30] , and Obo-edit [31] . We begin by listing some available open source biomedical ontologies ( Table 1 ) and bioinformatic databases ( Table 2 ).\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=1\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":9,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.79] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"in order to transmit entities to various ontologies. To reuse entities already present in other works, users are thus required to employ links rather than pure importations. Swoop does not currently support trading with shares, but a \\u201dsimple copy-paste \\u201d could provide a workaround.\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=6\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":10,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.235] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 9]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=9\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":11,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.245] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Fig. 7. OntoGraph relationships.\\n\\ngeneral characteristics and common knowledge regarding experimental studies, as well as to allow the communication among users, opening the opportunity to explore e-science concepts into the LS experimenta- tion domain. Potential future research directions would be to automate the process of creating the ontology. Beyond the creation of new on- tologies, another challenging area of work in the subject of ontologies is the evolution and mapping of current ontologies. Due to the cost of cre- ation, abstraction, and reusability, this is signi\\ufb01cant. Di\\ufb00erent types of ontologies can be derived from a core ontology to suit particular appli-\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=10\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":12,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.92] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"3.8. Software comparison\\n\\nWe now provide a general ODEs comparison. Table 3 collects gen- eral discussed tools features. Table 4 collects tools aspects of Usability, Scalability, Stability, Integration, Documentation and Originality. Each characteristic is evaluated with a score ranging from a minimum of 1 to a maximum of 3 yellow stars.\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=7\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":13,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.236] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 9]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=9\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":14,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.5] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Ontologies are used to support access to a multitude of databases that cover domains relevant information. Het- erogeneity and di\\ufb00erent semantics can be accessed by using structured texts and descriptions in a hierarchical concept de\\ufb01nition. We are interested in Life Sciences (LS) related ontologies including components taken from molecular biology, bioinformatics, physics, chemistry, medicine and other related areas. An Ontology comprises: (i) term connections, (ii) the identi\\ufb01cation of core concepts, (iii) data management, (iv) knowledge classi\\ufb01ca- tion and integration to collect key information. An ontology may be very useful in navigating through LS terms. This paper explores some available biomedical ontologies and frameworks. It describes the most common on- tology development environments (ODE): Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9, Topbraid Composer, Ontostudio, Fluent Editor, VocBench, Swoop and Obo-edit, to create ontologies from textual scienti\\ufb01c resources for LS plans. It also compares ontology\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=1\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":15,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.99] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"matter are available on its Standard (SE) and Maestro Editions (ME). Fluent editor provides two plug-ins viz., a Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 interoperability plug- e in and an Rplug-in using the ROntorionpakages to organize the ontology l b a content. VocBench3 support plugins which provide additional capabili- T\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=7\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":16,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.237] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 9]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=9\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":17,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.8] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"Ontologies are increasingly being employed to enhance understand- ing and transferability of knowledge in Life Science [1\\u20137] . Research groups are interested in developing ontologies [8] to arrange and con- nect data into searchable libraries [ 9 , 10 ], to establish frameworks of essential concepts (e.g., OWS framework [11] ), to share information, to use knowledge to minimize confusion [12] and to allocate the grow- ing volume and data multiplicity in biomedical research, chemistry, medicine, genomics, biology and pharmacology [13] . Further reasons for appreciating its utility are a wider understanding of the value of standardized vocabularies and codi\\ufb01ed semantics in various domains [8] and the development or extension of construction tools. To enable widespread access [14] and give users the ability to publish, explore, create and update, a few works have been published regarding pro- cedures [15\\u201318] plans, criteria, activities and methodologies adapted to build them. The state of\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=1\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":18,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.89] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"of fatal \\ufb02aws that could jeopardize accuracy. Checks are performed automatically during very speci\\ufb01c stages of the ontology\\u2019s life, such as when it is loaded, when text \\ufb01elds are changed and while using the Text Editor, that also keeps track of feedback on the actions taken. There are many di\\ufb00erent control studies, that range from the examination of the name of terms and associated IDs, to glancing for synonyms and dis- junction features. If a user needs to manually engage with the program control system he must utilize the Veri\\ufb01cation Manager. The window contains a list of all kinds of inspection allowed. Finally, we mention the idea of a cross product, also present in Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 and Swoop, but with a somewhat di\\ufb00erent meaning in this situation. In OBO-Edit Cross Prod- uct represents the intersection or relations among the classes that ensure the identi\\ufb01cation of all the hierarchical correspondences within the on- tology. In Prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 and Swoop,\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=6\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"attachments\":[{\"index\":19,\"type\":\"text/markdown\",\"title\":\"[0.238] DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf\",\"data\":\"[Image of the page 9]\",\"reference_url\":\"files/DpZGXdhaTxtaR67JyAHgDVkSP3Fo4nvV4FYCWNadE2Ln/ontologies.pdf#page=9\"}],\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"name\":\" [0.04s]\",\"status\":null}]}},\"finish_reason\":null,\"index\":0}],\"created\":1760528238,\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"usage\":null}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"4f3d3b3b-070a-452c-b89b-0280efa3d95b\",\"choices\":[{\"delta\":{\"custom_content\":{\"stages\":[{\"index\":6,\"status\":\"completed\"}